---
// Case Studies index. Hero framing is drawn selectively from the casebook's
// introduction ("learning engineering is a toolbox, not a single tool";
// "capability is a system parameter") to position LENS as an extension of
// learning engineering. Cases are grouped by topical part and shown as
// failure/success pairs, matching the book's per-part structure.
import ResourceAbout from "../../components/ResourceAbout.astro";
import FoundationCheck from "../../components/FoundationCheck.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import AskAI from "../../components/AskAI.astro";
import CaseIcon from "../../components/CaseIcon.astro";
import { CASE_ROLES } from "../../lib/lens";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

const cases = (await getCollection("case-studies", (c) => !c.data.draft)).sort(
  (a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order,
);

// Group into parts, preserving order; each part holds related cases.
const parts = new Map<number, { name: string; entries: typeof cases }>();
for (const c of cases) {
  const p = parts.get(c.data.partNumber) ?? { name: c.data.part, entries: [] };
  p.entries.push(c);
  parts.set(c.data.partNumber, p);
}
const grouped = [...parts.entries()].sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

// The main reading edition contains 51 historical chapters and a closing studio.
const CASEBOOK_PDF_URL = base + "capability-matters-casebook-draft.pdf";
---

<Base title="Case Studies · LENS" active="case-studies">

  <header class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="eyebrow">Case Studies</div>
      <h1>Case Studies: What happened, and what can we learn?</h1>
<ResourceAbout resource="case-library" content={{
        what: 'A source-linked collection of documented cases, grouped by domain to compare failures, interventions and capability designs. Each case preserves its evidence qualifications and scope limits.',
        how: ['Choose a domain and read two cases. Compare the system conditions, actions and outcomes before drawing a lesson.', 'Follow the sources and disclosures, then propose a new context where the explanation might fail. The linked casebook provides the wider reading edition.'],
        connection: 'Contrasting cases support conditional judgment about human and system performance. LENS asks which capability, information, authority or support changed what people could do; a historical association alone does not establish that a proposed intervention will work elsewhere.',
      }} />
      <p class="subhead">
        Compare what people could do, what the system made possible, and what
        mattered to the people affected. Some cases document learning engineering.
        Others show where it could directly address a failure, or help you
        distinguish a capability problem from a technical or governance problem.
      </p>
      <p class="grounding">
        <strong>{cases.length} selected cases across {grouped.length} domains.</strong>
        Drawn from <em>Capability Matters: A Casebook</em>. Download the revised
        reading edition below.
      </p>
      <p class="grounding"><a href="#why-lens-uses-a-casebook">Read the memo: Why LENS uses a casebook</a>.</p>
      <p class="grounding">To try a teaching simulation, visit <a href={base + "experiments/"}>Experiments</a>.
        To develop and test an intervention, explore <a href={base + "problems-to-prototypes/"}>Prototypes</a>.</p>

    </div>
  </header>

  <aside class="container" aria-label="Learning orientation"><p><a href="/learning-map/">Find your path through the site</a>. For a first reading, compare two cases from one domain: what did people need to do, how did the system support or constrain them, and what evidence supports your explanation?</p></aside>
  <div class="container">
    <FoundationCheck context="case" />
    <p><strong>How to read the cards:</strong> “What failed” and “What worked” describe the reported outcome in the case, not a judgment of the people involved. Paired cases invite comparison; they do not establish that one factor caused an outcome. Competency tags identify the LENS practices exercised, while topic domains identify the setting. Open a case to examine its sources and limits.</p>
    {grouped.map(([num, part]) => (
      <section class="part">
        <h2>
          <span class="pnum">{num}</span>
          {part.name}
        </h2>
        <div class="pair">
          {part.entries
            .sort((a, b) => Number(a.data.outcome === "success") - Number(b.data.outcome === "success") || a.data.order - b.data.order)
            .map((c) => (
              <article class={`card ${c.data.outcome}`}>
                <div class="card-head">
                  <CaseIcon slug={c.id} label={c.data.title} size={44} />
                  <span class="tag">{c.data.outcome === "fail" ? "What failed" : c.data.teachingRole === "capability-design" ? "Capability in practice" : "What worked"}</span>
                </div>
                <h3>
                  <a class="stretched" href={base + "case-studies/" + c.id + "/"}>{c.data.title}</a>
                </h3>
                <span class="year">{c.data.year}</span>
                <span class="case-role">{CASE_ROLES[c.data.teachingRole].label}</span>
                <p>{c.data.teaser}</p>
                <div class="card-foot">
                  <span class="more">Read the case →</span>
                  <AskAI
                    title={c.data.title}
                    year={c.data.year}
                    domains={c.data.domains}
                    outcome={c.data.outcome}
                    impact={c.data.impact}
                    leInsight={c.data.leInsight}
                    lensApproach={c.data.lensApproach}
                    lensAnchor={c.data.lensAnchor}
                    coi={c.data.coi}
                    evidenceFlag={c.data.evidenceFlag}
                    summary={c.data.summary}
                    competingReadings={c.data.competingReadings}
                    scopeLimit={c.data.scopeLimit}
                    teachingRole={c.data.teachingRole}
                    sources={c.data.sources}
                    variant="icon"
                  />
                </div>
              </article>
            ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}

    {cases.length === 0 && <p class="empty">No case studies yet — check back soon.</p>}

    <section id="why-lens-uses-a-casebook" aria-labelledby="casebook-memo-title">
      <h2 id="casebook-memo-title">Why LENS uses a casebook</h2>
      <p>The casebook puts the argument of <a href={base + "show-your-work/"}>Capability Matters</a> into practice. What people can accomplish depends on what they know and can do, and on what the system makes possible. The cases ask where a gap came from, what could change it, and what evidence would justify acting. Ask who benefits, who carries the burden, and who has the authority to act or contest a decision.</p>
      <p><a href={base + "case-studies/i-pass-handoff-bundle/"}>I-PASS</a> shows how resident practice, faculty feedback, and a structured handoff work together. In the <a href={base + "case-studies/inl-turbine-control-upgrade/"}>INL turbine-control case</a>, operators used a simulator to expose interface problems before formal validation. Failure cases let readers design and test a response to a documented gap while keeping the proposed response distinct from what happened.</p>
      <p><a href={base + "case-studies/rwanda-rapidsms/"}>Rwanda RapidSMS</a> shows why sending a referral and completing it require different evidence. The <a href={base + "case-studies/latin-american-learning-analytics/"}>Latin American Learning Analytics project</a> shows how local inquiry shaped advising tools and pilots. <a href={base + "case-studies/navy-surface-warfare-readiness-reform/"}>Navy Surface Warfare Readiness Reform</a> asks how to tell whether assessed skills persist and transfer to supervised work at sea.</p>
      <p>The <a href={base + "approach/"}>LENS approach</a> moves from Understand through Map, Design, Build, Instrument, Experiment, and Evaluate, then returns to Understand. Refine throughout. Pick a case, define the work, map what people and systems must provide, and specify a result that would make you revise the design.</p>
    </section>

    <aside class="casebook-download">
      <div class="cb-eyebrow">Draft · feedback welcome</div>
      <h2>Read the whole casebook</h2>
      <p>
        The {cases.length} cases above are a selection. The full reading edition of
        <em>Capability Matters: A Casebook</em> includes fifty-one historical
        chapters across seven parts and a closing studio. The November 2026
        revision distinguishes documented interventions from proposed responses
        and updates source attributions, contested findings and evidence limits.
      </p>
      <div class="cb-actions">
        <a class="cb-btn" href={CASEBOOK_PDF_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          Download the revised draft (PDF)
        </a>
        <a
          class="cb-feedback"
          href="mailto:wgr@jhu.edu?subject=Capability%20Matters%20casebook%20—%20feedback"
        >
          Send feedback →
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="cb-license">
        See the draft’s colophon for copyright and reuse information.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { background: var(--tint); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .hero .container { padding-top: 3rem; padding-bottom: 2rem; }
  .subhead { font-size: 1.08rem; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 42rem; }
  .grounding {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 42rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    padding: 0.8rem 1rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-2);
    background: var(--bg);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .grounding strong { color: var(--accent-2); font-weight: 600; }

  .part { margin: 2.6rem 0; }
  .part h2 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.7rem;
    font-size: 1.35rem;
    border: none;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
  .pnum {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--bg);
    background: var(--accent-2);
    padding: 0.1rem 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  .pair {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  .card {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding: 1.2rem 1.3rem 1.3rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-top-width: 3px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--ink);
    transition: box-shadow 0.15s ease, transform 0.15s ease;
  }
  .card:hover {
    box-shadow: 0 6px 20px rgba(26, 29, 26, 0.09);
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }
  .card.fail { border-top-color: #a33; }
  .card.success { border-top-color: #2f7a4f; }
  .tag {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.66rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .card.fail .tag { color: #7a2222; }
  .card.success .tag { color: #1f5c3a; }
  .card-head { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.7rem; }
  .case-role { color: var(--muted); font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.8rem; }
  /* Icon tile picks up the card's outcome hue so the family still reads
     fail/success at a glance, without a second coat of color on the glyph. */
  .card.fail :global(.case-icon) { color: #a33; }
  .card.success :global(.case-icon) { color: #2f7a4f; }
  .card h3 { font-size: 1.25rem; margin: 0; line-height: 1.2; }
  /* Stretched link: the title anchor covers the whole card so the entire
     card is clickable, while the AskAI control (z-indexed above) stays a
     separate, independently clickable link — no invalid nested anchors. */
  .card h3 .stretched { color: var(--ink); border: none; }
  .card h3 .stretched::after { content: ""; position: absolute; inset: 0; }
  .card:hover h3 .stretched { color: var(--accent); }
  .year { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.75rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .card p {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0.2rem;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .card-foot {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    margin-top: auto;
    padding-top: 0.6rem;
  }
  .more {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .card-foot :global(.ask-ai) { position: relative; z-index: 1; }
  .empty { color: var(--muted); font-style: italic; padding: 3rem 0; }

  /* Casebook download — set apart from the case grid with the tint panel so it
     reads as a page-level offer, not another case card. */
  .casebook-download {
    margin: 3.4rem 0 3rem;
    padding: 1.8rem 2rem 1.6rem;
    background: var(--tint);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .cb-eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.07em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .casebook-download h2 {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    border: none;
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0.6rem;
  }
  .casebook-download p {
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 44rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .cb-actions {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem 1.4rem;
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0.9rem;
  }
  .cb-btn {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--bg);
    background: var(--accent);
    padding: 0.6rem 1.1rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    border: none;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  .cb-btn:hover { background: var(--accent-2); }
  .cb-feedback {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.88rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .cb-license {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .pair { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
